(2) are the WORST-j* hard clusters near-AP?  (evenness ~1 <=> AP-like)
  k=11, V=157:  top hard clusters by j*:
     j*= 3  evenness=1.541  near-AP  E=(0, 9, 19, 40, 59, 73, 82)...
     j*= 3  evenness=1.541  near-AP  E=(0, 7, 16, 34, 53, 70, 80)...
     j*= 3  evenness=1.471  near-AP  E=(0, 7, 26, 43, 55, 70, 82)...
     j*= 3  evenness=1.401  near-AP  E=(0, 16, 20, 38, 58, 72, 89)...
     corr(j*, evenness) = -0.129  (negative => high j* = more even = near-AP)
  k=13, V=185:  top hard clusters by j*:
     j*= 5  evenness=1.757  lumpy  E=(0, 25, 31, 50, 56, 67, 78)...
     j*= 5  evenness=1.757  lumpy  E=(0, 14, 19, 43, 68, 73, 83)...
     j*= 4  evenness=1.827  lumpy  E=(0, 25, 51, 66, 79, 94, 103)...
     j*= 4  evenness=1.827  lumpy  E=(0, 4, 30, 34, 56, 72, 88)...
     corr(j*, evenness) = -0.020  (negative => high j* = more even = near-AP)

=> if maxj* <= bound everywhere (0 fails) and high-j* <=> near-AP (neg corr),
   then general j*=O(k) reduces to: [hard => quasi-even (near-AP)] + [mac-mini AP clustering].
